e $\\rho_1$ has $\\sigma - \\Re\\rho_1 < \\min(D, D_*)$.} Choose a closed interval $J$ around $\\Im\\rho_1$ such that $|\\sigma + i\\tau - \\rho_1| < \\min(D, D_*)$ for all $\\tau \\in J$. Only finitely many poles lie within distance $\\min(D, D_*)$ of the compact segment $\\{\\sigma + i\\tau \\mid \\tau \\in J\\}$. Two of them are equidistant from $\\sigma + i\\tau$ for at most one $\\tau$: if their imaginary parts differ, equality of the squared distances is a linear equation in $\\tau$ with nonzero slope; if their imaginary parts agree, their real parts differ, and since both lie left of the line their distances to it differ. Choose $\\tau \\in J$ avoiding these finitely many values and put $z = \\sigma + i\\tau$. Then $z$ has a unique nearest pole $\\rho$, at distance $d \\le |z - \\rho_1| < \\min(D, D_*)$. Choose $r$ with $d < r < \\min(D, D_*)$ such that $\\rho$ is the only pole in $\\overline B(z, r)$. The disc $\\overline B(\\sigma, r)$ contains no pole, as $r < D$. By Step 1,\n\\begin{align*}\n|A_k(z)| + \\Re A_k(\\sigma) &\\ge |A_k(z)| - |A_k(\\sigma)| \\\\\n&\\ge |c_\\rho|\\, d^{-k-1} - O(r^{-k}) \\ge \\frac{|c_\\rho|}{2d}\\, d^{-k}\n\\end{align*}\nfor all large $k$, because $d < r$. This is the claim with $c = |c_\\rho|/(2d)$. We cannot simply take $z = \\sigma$: there the conjugate poles $\\rho_1$ and $\\bar\\rho_1$ are equally close, and their contributions to $A_k(\\sigma)$ may cancel. See the \\hyperref[8JT8]{picture of case (a)}.\n\n\\emph{Case (b): every pole has $\\sigma - \\Re\\rho \\ge \\min(D, D_*)$.} For $\\rho_0$ this gives $\\min(D, D_*) \\le \\sigma - \\Re\\rho_0 < D_*$, so $D < D_*$. A pole $\\rho$ nearest to $\\sigma$ satisfies $D = |\\sigma - \\rho| \\ge \\sigma - \\Re\\rho \\ge D$. So equality holds, $\\rho$ is real, and $\\rho = \\beta = \\sigma - D$; in particular $\\beta$ is the only pole at distance $D$ from $\\sigma$. Its residue $c_\\beta$ is positive by the \\hyperref[8JS7]{corollary on real poles}. Choose $r$ with $D < r < D_*$ such that $\\beta$ is the only pole in $\\overline B(\\sigma, r)$. By Step 1, $A_k(\\sigma) = c_\\beta D^{-k-1} + O(r^{-k})$, so with $\\tau = 0$\n\\begin{align*}\n|A_k(\\sigma)| + \\Re A_k(\\sigma) &\\ge 2\\Re A_k(\\sigma) \\\\\n&\\ge 2c_\\beta D^{-k-1} - O(r^{-k}) \\ge \\frac{c_\\beta}{D}\\, D^{-k}\n\\end{align*}\nfor all large $k$. This is the claim with $d = D$ and $c = c_\\beta/D$. Here the sign of $c_\\beta$ is essential: if $c_\\beta$ were negative, $|A_k(\\sigma)| + \\Re A_k(\\sigma)$ could be small. See the \\hyperref[8JT9]{picture of case (b)}.","status":"ai_generated","generated_by":"claude","generator_detail":"claude-opus-5-5","reviewed_by":null,"review_notes":null,"position":1}', 'claude');
